.TH FAIL2ZIG 1 "2026-09-21" "fail2zig 0.4.2" "User Commands"
.
.SH NAME
fail2zig \- intrusion prevention daemon and its administration commands
.
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B fail2zig
[\fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-foreground\fR]
[\fB\-\-validate\-config\fR]
[\fB\-\-import\-config\fR [\fIsource\-dir\fR]]
[\fB\-\-import\-output\fR \fIpath\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig
[\fIglobal flags\fR]
\fIcommand\fR
[\fIargs\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig rule-test
(\fB\-\-file\fR \fIpath\fR | \fB\-\-record\fR \fIline\fR | \fB\-\-journal\fR [\fImatch\fR...])
(\fB\-\-service\fR \fIname\fR | \fB\-\-rule\-file\fR \fIpath\fR)
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig migrate
\fBinspect\fR|\fBsnapshot\fR|\fBplan\fR|\fBvalidate\fR|\fBcutover\fR|\fBstatus\fR|\fBrollback\fR
[\fIoptions\fR]
.br
.B fail2zig
\fB\-\-version\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR
.
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B fail2zig
is a host-based intrusion prevention daemon. It reads log files or the systemd
journal, matches compiled service filters or bounded native rules, and applies
temporary bans through nftables (direct netlink), ipset or iptables.
.PP
One executable provides every entry point. Without a command it starts the daemon;
with a command it administers a running daemon over its Unix socket;
.B rule-test
and
.B migrate
work offline.
.PP
The daemon runs in the foreground only and is designed for a
.BR systemd (1)
unit of
.BR Type=notify :
it sends
.B READY=1
only after configuration, storage, every source and enforcing protection are admitted,
.B RELOADING=1
then
.B READY=1
around a live reload, and
.B STOPPING=1
on exit.
.B SIGHUP
applies a validated configuration generation live;
.B SIGUSR1
reopens
.B global.log_target
after rotation;
.B SIGTERM
and
.B SIGINT
stop cleanly. No socket unit is shipped and
.B LISTEN_FDS
is not read.
.
.SH DAEMON OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Configuration file. Default:
.IR /etc/fail2zig/config.toml .
Unknown keys are rejected with line and column context.
.TP
.B \-\-foreground
Run in the foreground. Accepted for clarity; it is the only mode.
.TP
.B \-\-validate\-config
Load and validate the configuration, print the result and exit without starting
sources, touching the firewall or writing state.
.B \-\-test\-config
is an alias.
.TP
.BI \-\-import\-config " [source-dir]"
Translate a fail2ban configuration tree (default
.IR /etc/fail2ban )
into a native configuration and a protected provenance manifest, then exit.
Unsupported semantics are marked
.B compatibility_pending
rather than approximated.
.TP
.BI \-\-import\-output " path"
Destination of the imported configuration. Default:
.IR /etc/fail2zig/config.toml .
.TP
.BR \-\-version ", " \-V
Print the version and exit without contacting the daemon.
.TP
.BR \-\-help ", " \-h
Print usage and exit.
.
.SH GLOBAL FLAGS (ADMINISTRATION)
.TP
.BI \-\-socket " path"
Unix socket. Default:
.IR /run/fail2zig/fail2zig.sock .
.TP
.BI \-\-output " format"
.BR table " (default), " json " or " plain
(tab-separated).
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Disable ANSI colour.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " ms"
Command timeout. Default 5000. Expiry exits with class 3.
.
.SH COMMANDS
Members of the
.B fail2zig
group may run the read-only commands through the 0660 socket. Mutations
.RB ( ban ", " unban ", " reload ", " "history reset" ", " jail )
require uid 0 or the daemon uid, verified with
.BR SO_PEERCRED .
.TP
.B status
Daemon summary: version, protection state
.RB ( active ", " log-only ", " mixed " or " "DEGRADED (cause)" ),
backend, active and total bans, storage phase, generation and worker/clock flags.
Diagnostic rows show known storage, source and firewall causes. A missing cause is
reported as unknown; it does not establish healthy protection. The recovery retry
deadline is in absolute monotonic milliseconds, not a remaining delay.
Plain output adds named diagnostic keys; JSON fields are additive and optional.
Table output uses a compact protection summary. Add
.B --details
to include identifiers and additional counters; failures remain visible without it.
.TP
.B jails
One row per configured jail: enabled, paused, health, active bans, thresholds,
effective action, whether it enforces, and its source. Table output includes the
source failure cause and captured journalctl exit/signal metadata. Raw child stderr
is not displayed. Plain output retains its 11-column layout.
Table output shows state, source health, enforcement and bans; add
.B --details
for thresholds and effective action.
.TP
.BI list " [--jail name] [--details]"
Active bans, optionally for one jail. Table output distinguishes
confirmed and pending intent, permanent leases and unknown expiry; narrow terminals
use stacked rows. Existing plain columns and JSON fields retain their meaning.
The --details flag adds ban counts to the table view.
.TP
.BI "firewall show" " [--limit 1..256] [--cursor token] [--details]"
Read the last sampled fail2zig-owned
kernel protection in the daemon's network namespace. Native nftables inspection
does not require the nft executable. The query performs no firewall refresh or mutation.
Observation age and the latest attempt distinguish retained data from a failed
readback. Unavailable, absent and owned-but-empty are separate states; a successful
query exit does not prove installed protection or packet reachability.
With --details, show the tables, chains, sets and attachment rules verified in
the retained observation, plus the location of each sampled entry. This normalized
owned view is not a raw ruleset dump. Structures retained after a failed check are
stale observations, not current-state claims. Structural metadata from older
daemons may be unavailable. JSON adds nullable structure and per-item placement;
--details does not change plain or JSON output for any command.
At most 256 entries are sampled from a complete readback. The default page size is
64. JSON reports observed_total, sample_count and sample_truncated separately from
observation_complete. next_cursor pages only the retained sample; use the same limit.
A replaced observation, daemon restart or 60-second cursor lifetime requires
restarting pagination without a cursor. No jail filter or forced refresh is provided.
Timeouts are sampled values; unexpected entries do not imply desired or confirmed bans.
.TP
.BI ban " ip --jail name [--duration seconds] [--scope host|net cidr]"
Manual ban. Exactly one
.B \-\-jail
is required.
.B \-\-scope net
bans a network and needs a backend with set support (nftables or ipset).
A log-only jail rejects manual bans.
.TP
.BI unban " ip --jail name [--scope host|net cidr]"
Release a manual or automatic ban in one jail.
.TP
.B reload
Propose the current configuration file as a new generation. Outcomes:
.BR noop ", " applied ", " rejected " or " restart_required
(the restart-only keys are listed in
.BR reasons ).
Live keys: per-jail
.BR maxretry ", " bantime ", " bantime_kind ", " bantime_increment ,
global
.BR log_level ,
and the contents of a custom jail's
.BR ignore_file .
.TP
.BI history " [--jail name] [--limit 1..256] [--cursor token]"
Page through confirmed ban history;
.B next_cursor
continues a listing.
.TP
.BI "history reset " "ip (--jail name | --all)"
Reset the durable history for one address.
.TP
.BI "jail enable|disable|pause|resume " name
Administer one jail. Disabling releases its effects; pausing keeps them.
.TP
.B config
The effective configuration and its generation. Paths and address lists are
redacted unless the caller is an administrator.
.TP
.B version
Daemon version over the socket (unlike
.BR \-\-version ).
Table output combines matching client and daemon versions on one
line and labels each separately when they differ. Plain and JSON output retain
separate client and daemon fields.
.TP
.BI completions " bash|zsh|fish"
Shell completion script on stdout.
.TP
.BI help " [command]"
General or per-command help.
.
.SH RULE-TEST
.B fail2zig rule-test
evaluates one input against one builtin service or one native rule file with the
daemon's own decoder, time admission and detectors. It opens no state store, no
firewall and no daemon socket.
.TP
Input (exactly one)
.BI \-\-file " path"
(regular file, streamed, at most 64 MiB, lines up to 64 KiB),
.BI \-\-record " line" ,
or
.B \-\-journal
[\fImatch\fR...] through the fixed
.BR journalctl (1)
invocation.
.TP
Rule (exactly one)
.BI \-\-service " name"
(one of the compiled services) or
.BI \-\-rule\-file " path"
(bounded native JSON rule, at most 4096 bytes).
.TP
Options
.BI \-\-config " path"
[\fB\-\-jail\fR \fIname\fR] resolves timestamp, time zone and ignore settings from a
native configuration;
.BI \-\-timestamp " iso8601|syslog|epoch_seconds|common_log|undated" ,
.BI \-\-time\-zone " id" ,
.BI \-\-tz\-offset " minutes" ,
.BI \-\-year " yyyy" ,
.BI \-\-encoding " utf8|ascii|latin1|utf16le|utf16be|utf32le|utf32be" ,
.BI \-\-identity " raw|dns"
(\fBdns\fR resolves hostnames only through
.BI \-\-dns\-server " ip:port"
with a bounded timeout; the default never touches the network),
.BI \-\-ignore " cidr" ,
.BI \-\-max\-lines " n" ,
.BI \-\-limit " n"
(samples, default 20),
.BI \-\-output " json|table" ,
.BR \-\-print\-lines ,
.BI \-\-now " epoch-seconds" ,
.BI \-\-window " seconds" .
.PP
Samples report the outcome
.RB ( match ", " miss ", " ignore ", " reject ),
the reason, the extracted identity, the event time and the time disposition.
Raw lines are included only with
.BR \-\-print\-lines .
Exit 0 when the input was processed (even with zero matches), 1 when the rule,
configuration or input was refused, 2 on usage errors.
.
.SH MIGRATE
Read-only preparation:
.B inspect
(\fB\-\-source\-dir\fR),
.B snapshot
(\fB\-\-source\-db\fR \fB\-\-staging\-dir\fR),
.B plan
(\fB\-\-source\-dir\fR, optional \fB\-\-source\-db\fR with \fB\-\-staging\-dir\fR,
\fB\-\-out\fR, \fB\-\-continuity lossless|reset-replay\fR, \fB\-\-replay\-window\fR,
\fB\-\-runtime\-socket\fR)
and
.B validate
(\fB\-\-plan\fR). The staging directory must be private (0700) and non-volatile.
.PP
.B cutover
(\fB\-\-plan \-\-state\-file \-\-staging\-dir \-\-backend nftables|ipset|iptables\fR
[\fB\-\-socket\fR] [\fB\-\-run\-id\fR]) stages the destination offline with the daemon
stopped and drives activation and kernel verification inside the daemon. It is
journaled: an unreachable socket exits 3 and prints the exact
.B \-\-run\-id
command to resume, and a rerun classifies the journal instead of replaying.
.B status
reports a run.
.PP
.B rollback
(\fB\-\-plan \-\-state\-file \-\-staging\-dir \-\-backend \-\-run\-id\fR
[\fB\-\-socket\fR] [\fB\-\-source\-verified\fR]) reverses an activated run in two
journaled phases. First it validates the staging recovery point against the plan's
snapshot and fingerprints the live source (WAL-aware); a mismatch is
.B rollback_failed
(exit 4) with nothing written, an environment failure is
.B operational_failure
(exit 1) and resumable. It then carries the post-cutover deltas back, moves the original
with its journal siblings to
.IR <source>.pre-rollback-<run16> ,
renames the fsync'd derived database into place, records a
.I restored-<run16>
marker in the staging directory and exits 3 with the next command. Then, with
.BR \-\-source\-verified ,
the source control socket present and a running
.B fail2ban-server
process, it releases the migrated scopes from the daemon: exit 0 with state
.BR rolled_back ;
partial (4), uncertain (5) or refused (1) releases leave the step open; an unreachable
daemon exits 3. See
.IR docs/operations/command-migration.md .
.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Every command returns one of the frozen classes.
.TP
.B 0
Success; every claimed effect was observed.
.TP
.B 1
Valid request rejected by the daemon or the local operation (fail-closed startup,
log-only jail, zero jails imported, refused rule or input).
.TP
.B 2
Usage, argument, configuration parse or validation failure.
.TP
.B 3
Daemon or transport unavailable (no socket, refused, timeout).
.TP
.B 4
A durable effect was applied but kernel confirmation is incomplete.
.TP
.B 5
A durable effect may have been applied and could not be established.
.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/fail2zig/config.toml
Configuration; see
.BR fail2zig.toml (5).
.TP
.I /run/fail2zig/fail2zig.sock
Administration socket, mode 0660, group
.BR fail2zig .
The parent directory must be owned by root or the daemon uid with no group or
other write bit; the daemon refuses to serve otherwise.
.TP
.I /var/lib/fail2zig/state.bin
Durable SQLite state: records, decisions, effects, history and source positions.
The parent and database must be owned by the daemon UID, with no group or
other write access. This is not the legacy v0.3.0 binary state format.
.TP
.I global.log_target
Optional append-only log file (mode 0640) instead of stderr; reopened on
.BR SIGUSR1 .
.
.SH SERVICE ACCOUNT AND UPGRADE
The shipped unit runs as the non-login user and group
.BR fail2zig ,
retaining CAP_NET_ADMIN, CAP_NET_RAW and CAP_DAC_READ_SEARCH for firewall and log access.
Root or the daemon UID may mutate protection; service-group membership grants
socket monitoring only. CAP_NET_RAW is required by the iptables ipset extension
and also permits raw IP sockets. HTTP runs in the same process with the same capabilities.
.PP
Stop every daemon and state writer and preserve configuration, executable and
coherent state backups before upgrading. The installer does not enable, start
or restart services. It changes ownership only for the default native SQLite
directory, database and existing WAL/SHM files; active writers, unsafe paths,
legacy binary state and automatic custom-path upgrades are refused.
.PP
No automatic conversion of v0.3.0 binary state is provided. Starting a fresh
native database does not preserve its saved bans, counters, history or source
positions. Supported fail2ban schema-4 SQLite migration is a separate workflow.
Native destination migration must run as the service UID with the required
capabilities and caller-owned private staging, with the destination daemon
stopped. See docs/operations/command-migration.md and migration-continuity.md.
.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B NOTIFY_SOCKET
Set by
.BR systemd (1)
for
.BR Type=notify ;
absent means notifications are disabled.
.TP
.B PATH
Searched for
.B ipset
and
.B iptables
when that backend is selected.
.B journalctl
is invoked at
.I /usr/bin/journalctl
only.
.PP
No other variables are read by the daemon or the administration commands.
.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
sudo fail2zig --validate-config --config /etc/fail2zig/config.toml
sudo fail2zig status
fail2zig list --jail sshd --output json
sudo fail2zig ban 192.0.2.10 --jail sshd --duration 3600
sudo fail2zig reload
fail2zig rule-test --file /var/log/auth.log --service sshd --tz-offset 0
fail2zig completions bash > /etc/bash_completion.d/fail2zig
.fi
.
